---
/**
 * The wordmark as inline SVG: "lion" in the inherited text colour, "ui" in the accent colour.
 *
 * It used to be public/lionui-text.svg loaded through <img>, with both colours baked in (white and
 * #FFC107) plus an `invert(1) hue-rotate(180deg)` filter for light mode. Two measured defects came
 * out of that: in the header the filter turned the accent into rgb(124,62,0) - a brown tint - and in
 * the footer, where no filter applied, the white half was invisible on the light page (255,255,255
 * pixels x2804). Inline, both halves follow the theme on their own and the filter can go.
 */
---
<svg
  class="logo-text-svg"
  width="86"
  height="36"
  viewBox="0 0 86 36"
  fill="none"
  role="img"
  aria-label="lion ui"
  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
>
  <text x="0" y="28" class="wordmark-glyphs" fill="currentColor">lıon</text>
  <text x="54" y="28" class="wordmark-glyphs wordmark-accent" fill="var(--accent-yellow)">uı</text>
</svg>

<style>
  .logo-text-svg {
    height: 36px;
    width: auto;
    display: block;
  }

  .wordmark-glyphs {
    font-family: var(--font-family);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 32px;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  /*
   * Outline on the accent half only. "lion" is already #111111 on light chrome (16:1), so it needs
   * nothing; "ui" is #FFC107, which is 1.63:1 and stops being readable as the bar gets lighter, so
   * paint-order keeps the stroke behind the fill, so the letters stay yellow and the outline only
   * separates them from the background. In dark mode the stroke is transparent - the accent there
   * sits on near-black, where a dark outline would just blur it.
   *
   * light-dark() takes colours only: as a length the declaration is invalid and gets dropped.
   */
  .wordmark-accent {
    paint-order: stroke fill;
    stroke: light-dark(rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55), transparent);
    stroke-width: 1.5px;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }
</style>
